{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789457495,"nonce":"Heea7p72FYm3gYKgBaSzmvBKcRk=","title":"Release analysis: a list parameter counts as Copied, so every call taking a list is an escape","message":"A list travels as its header struct, and `param_ownership` in lowering maps a struct-typed parameter to `ParamOwnership::Copied`; the drop pass treats only `Borrowed`/`BorrowedMut` parameters as leaving the caller's claim intact, so a call that passes a list to any non-inlined function is an escape. Whether a temporary list gets released then depends on whether the inliner happened to absorb the callee: `zb_dict_from_pairs` returned owned storage while `zb_dict_update` was inlined into it, and stopped the moment the loop moved into a helper, leaving every dict literal's pair list to the collector (about 96 bytes a row on bench_records, 495k blocks by the sixth collection).\n\nWorkaround landed with the dict index: the builtins DSL has `borrowed(name, ty)`, which sets `ParamOwnership::Borrowed` explicitly, and the dict functions use it for the dict and index parameters they only read or edit in place.\n\nThe general fix is inference: a parameter is kept when it reaches a store as a value, a return, a `Shared`/`Owned` parameter of a callee, or an extern that keeps it, and borrowed otherwise; computed to a fixed point alongside `returns_param`. Then the DSL marker becomes a check rather than the source of truth.","files":null}]}